Here is the kind of code that throws at runtime. Nothing warns you at compile time.

```java
public String cityOf(Order order) {
    // Any link in the chain can be null
    return order.getCustomer().getAddress().getCity().toUpperCase();
}
```

If `getCustomer()` returns `null`, the very next `.getAddress()` call throws a `NullPointerException` immediately.

The fix keeps every step safe. `Optional` chains the calls and falls back when anything is missing.

```java
public String cityOf(Order order) {
    Objects.requireNonNull(order, "order must not be null");
    return Optional.ofNullable(order.getCustomer())
            .map(Customer::getAddress)
            .map(Address::getCity)
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .orElse("UNKNOWN");
}
```

Now a `null` anywhere in the chain lands on `orElse("UNKNOWN")` instead of crashing the request.

public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;

    public String getCustomerCity(Long orderId) {
        // Strategy 1: Traditional null check
        var order = orderRepository.findById(orderId);
        if (order == null) {
            return "Unknown";
        }

        // Strategy 2: Optional prevents NPE in chained calls
        return Optional.ofNullable(order.getCustomer())
                .map(Customer::getAddress)
                .map(Address::getCity)
                .orElse("Unknown");
    }

    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        // Strategy 3: Fail fast with requireNonNull
        Objects.requireNonNull(order, "Order cannot be null");
        Objects.requireNonNull(order.getCustomer(), "Customer cannot be null");

        double total = 0;
        for (var item : order.getItems()) {
            total += item.getPrice(); // simple loop avoids unboxing surprises
        }
    }
}

A loop with the wrong comparison runs past the end of the array.

```java
public void printAll(String[] names) {
    // BUG: <= lets i reach names.length
    for (int i = 0; i <= names.length; i++) {
        System.out.println(names[i]); // throws when i == names.length
    }
}
```

On the final pass `i` equals `names.length`, so `names[i]` is out of bounds and throws.

Strict less-than keeps the loop inside the array, and the enhanced for loop removes the index entirely.

```java
public void printAll(String[] names) {
    for (int i = 0; i < names.length; i++) {   // strict <
        System.out.println(names[i]);
    }
    for (String name : names) {                // no index to mismanage
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
```

With `i < names.length`, the last index visited is `names.length - 1` — exactly the last element.

public class ArrayProcessor {

    public void processOrders(String[] orderIds) {
        // WRONG: classic off-by-one error
        // for (int i = 0; i <= orderIds.length; i++) {
        //     System.out.println(orderIds[i]); // crashes at i == length
        // }

        // CORRECT: traditional loop with proper bounds
        for (int i = 0; i < orderIds.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(orderIds[i]);
        }

        // BETTER: enhanced for loop eliminates index entirely
        for (var orderId : orderIds) {
            System.out.println(orderId);
        }
    }

    public String getLastOrder(String[] orders) {
        if (orders.length == 0) {
            return "No orders";
        }
        // Last element is at length - 1, not length
        return orders[orders.length - 1];
    }

    public String safeGet(int[] values, int index) {
        // Defensive bounds checking
        if (index < 0 || index >= values.length) {
            return "Index out of bounds: " + index;
        }
        return "Value: " + values[index];
    }
}

Casting straight from `Object` assumes a type the value may not have.

```java
public int lengthOf(Object value) {
    // Compiles, but throws if value is not a String
    String text = (String) value;
    return text.length();
}
```

Call `lengthOf(42)` and the `(String) value` cast throws a `ClassCastException` at runtime.

Java 16 pattern matching tests the type and binds the variable in one safe move.

```java
public int lengthOf(Object value) {
    if (value instanceof String text) {  // test and bind together
        return text.length();
    }
    return 0; // not a String — no cast, no crash
}
```

The `instanceof` guard means the cast only runs when the object truly is a `String`.

public class PaymentProcessor {

    public void processPayment(Object payment) {
        // WRONG: blind casting without type check
        // CreditCardPayment cc = (CreditCardPayment) payment; // throws if payment is not CreditCardPayment

        // OLD WAY: instanceof check then cast
        if (payment instanceof CreditCardPayment) {
            CreditCardPayment cc = (CreditCardPayment) payment;
            System.out.println("Processing card: " + cc.getCardNumber());
        }

        // JAVA 16+: pattern matching eliminates redundant cast
        if (payment instanceof CreditCardPayment cc) {
            System.out.println("Processing card: " + cc.getCardNumber());
        } else if (payment instanceof BankTransfer bt) {
            System.out.println("Processing transfer: " + bt.getAccountNumber());
        }
    }

    // BEST: use generics to prevent ClassCastException at compile time
    public <T extends Payment> void processTypedPayment(T payment) {
        payment.process(); // no cast needed, type is known
    }

    public void demonstrateProblem(List rawList) {
        rawList.add("text");
        rawList.add(123);
        // Integer num = (Integer) rawList.get(0); // throws ClassCastException at runtime
    }
}

Trim the text first, then catch the failure and return a default.

```java
public int quantityFrom(String input) {
    if (input == null) return 0;
    try {
        return Integer.parseInt(input.trim());
    } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
        return 0; // sensible default for bad input
    }
}
```

Now `" 5 "` parses cleanly, and `"five"` returns `0` instead of crashing the caller.

public class InputValidator {

    public int parseQuantity(String input) {
        // Pattern 1: try-catch with default value
        try {
            return Integer.parseInt(input);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.err.println("Invalid quantity: " + input);
            return 0; // sensible default
        }
    }

    public Optional<Integer> parseUserId(String input) {
        // Pattern 2: return Optional for caller to handle
        try {
            return Optional.of(Integer.parseInt(input));
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            return Optional.empty();
        }
    }

    public double parsePrice(String input) {
        // Pattern 3: validate before parsing
        if (input == null || input.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Price cannot be empty");
        }

        try {
            var price = Double.parseDouble(input.trim());
            if (price < 0) { // business validation
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Price cannot be negative");
            }
            return price;
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid price format: " + input);
        }
    }
}

A recursive method that never stops calling itself exhausts the stack.

```java
public int factorial(int n) {
    // BUG: nothing stops the recursion
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}
```

Each call pushes another frame, and after roughly a thousand calls the stack runs dry and throws `StackOverflowError`.

A base case ends the recursion, and an iterative version avoids deep stacks entirely.

```java
public int factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;        // base case stops recursion
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

public int factorialLoop(int n) {
    int result = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) result *= i; // no frames pile up
    return result;
}
```

The loop handles huge inputs that the recursive version could never reach.

public class RecursionExample {

    // WRONG: infinite recursion, no base case
    public int badFactorial(int n) {
        return n * badFactorial(n - 1); // never stops, StackOverflowError after ~1000 calls
    }

    // CORRECT: proper base case terminates recursion
    public int goodFactorial(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) { // base case: stop recursion
            return 1;
        }
        return n * goodFactorial(n - 1);
    }

    // EVEN BETTER: iterative approach avoids stack depth entirely
    public int iterativeFactorial(int n) {
        int result = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            result *= i;
        }
        return result; // no stack frames accumulated
    }

    // ACCIDENTAL: indirect recursion can also cause it
    public void methodA() {
        methodB();
    }

    public void methodB() {
        methodA(); // A calls B calls A calls B... StackOverflowError
    }
}

Removing from a list while a for-each loop walks it breaks the hidden iterator.

```java
public void dropExpired(List<Order> orders) {
    for (Order o : orders) {
        if (o.isExpired()) {
            orders.remove(o); // throws ConcurrentModificationException
        }
    }
}
```

The for-each loop uses a hidden iterator; `orders.remove(o)` changes the list behind its back, so the next pass throws.

public class OrderProcessor {

    public void cleanupOrders(List<Order> orders) {
        // WRONG: modifying list during for-each loop
        // for (var order : orders) {
        //     if (order.isExpired()) {
        //         orders.remove(order); // throws ConcurrentModificationException
        //     }
        // }

        // SOLUTION 1: use Iterator.remove (single element removal)
        var iterator = orders.iterator();
        while (iterator.hasNext()) {
            var order = iterator.next();
            if (order.isExpired()) {
                iterator.remove(); // safe: removes through iterator
            }
        }
    }

    public void removeInvalidOrders(List<Order> orders) {
        // SOLUTION 2: removeIf with predicate (Java 8+)
        orders.removeIf(order -> order.getTotal() < 0);
    }

    public void cancelPendingOrders(List<Order> orders) {
        // SOLUTION 3: collect items to remove, then remove in second pass
        var toRemove = orders.stream()
                .filter(order -> order.getStatus().equals("PENDING"))
                .toList();

        orders.removeAll(toRemove); // bulk removal after iteration
    }
}
